//Polymorphism

Polymorphism means one name/interface can have multiple forms or behaviors.

The word literally means “many forms.”

Example:

class Animal {
public:
    void sound() {
        cout << "Animal sound";
    }
};

class Dog : public Animal {
public:
    void sound() {
        cout << "Bark";
    }
};

class Cat : public Animal {
public:
    void sound() {
        cout << "Meow";
    }
};

Here the function name is the same:

sound()

But it behaves differently:

Dog → Bark
Cat → Meow

That's polymorphism.

Two main types

1. Compile-time polymorphism

Function overloading
Operator overloading

2. Runtime polymorphism

Function overriding
Virtual functions

We'll study compile-time and runtime polymorphism separately next.

Simple definition:

Polymorphism = the ability of the same interface/function name to behave differently in different situations.